cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "Squarefree integers dividing the product of their shifted prime divisors"
message: "If you use this work, please cite it as below."
type: dataset
authors:
  - family-names: "Ellena Godoy"
    given-names: "Jorge"
abstract: >-
  For an integer b, let S_b be the set of squarefree integers n dividing the
  product of (p + b) over the primes p of n. This interpolates two classical
  objects: b = 1 gives the squarefree part of n | sigma(n), whose only member
  above 1 is 6, and b = -1 gives n | phi(n). For odd positive b every prime of
  every member is at most b + 2, so S_b is finite. Membership depends only on b
  modulo n, and the number of residue classes of b for which a given squarefree
  n belongs is exactly the product over p | n of the number of distinct residues
  q mod p with q | n; this is what shows the count |S_b| is not a function of b
  through any elementary formula, since the local conditions couple b with the
  whole prime structure of n. For two prime factors the condition closes to
  pq | b(b+p+q), equivalently b congruent to phi(pq) - 1 modulo pq when neither
  prime divides b, which factors as (up-1)(uq-1) = ub+1 and turns the count into
  a factorization problem. The squarefree members are closed under least common
  multiple, so S_b has a maximum that every member divides. Complete counts are
  given for the 1001 odd b up to 2001. The case b = 1 is classical and is not
  claimed; all results are verified by independent implementations and
  cross-checked against OEIS A187778.
keywords:
  - "number theory"
  - "arithmetic functions"
  - "squarefree integers"
  - "shifted primes"
  - "sum of divisors"
  - "Dedekind psi function"
  - "Chinese remainder theorem"
  - "integer sequences"
license: CC-BY-4.0
repository-code: "https://github.com/jorgell23-sys/squarefree-shifted-prime-products"
